{"message": "Deep and focused, clearly. I'd rather spend an hour on one question and come out with something I didn't have at the start than touch twenty subjects and leave each one at the level of a first impression.

The reason is that most interesting things have a second and third layer that you only reach by staying put. Take a question like whether a city should cap rents. The first pass gives you the standard arguments on both sides — supply, affordability, landlords. That's fine, but it's the stuff you could get from any summary. The real content shows up when you keep pushing: what happened in Berlin versus Vienna versus Stockholm, why the same policy produced different results, what the enforcement actually looked like. You only get there if you don't wander off at minute ten.

Jumping between subjects feels productive because there's a lot of motion, but it's mostly motion. I'll take one thread pulled all the way through."}
